,title,link,text,language,family
0,Siouan languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siouan_languages,"Siouan (/ ˈsuːən / SOO-ən), also known as Siouan-Catawban (/ ˌsuːən kəˈtɔː.bən / SOO-ən kə-TAW-bən), is a language family of North America located primarily in the Great Plains, Ohio and Mississippi valleys and southeastern North America with a few other languages in the east.",Iowa-Oto,Siouan-Catawban
1,Iowa people - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iowa_people,"The Iowa, also known as Ioway or Báxoje (Iowa-Oto: Báxoje ich'é, ""grey snow people""), [3] are a Native American tribe. Historically, they spoke a Chiwere, which was a Siouan-Catawban language.",Iowa-Oto,Siouan-Catawban
2,Siouan-Catawban Language Family - aaanativearts.com,https://www.aaanativearts.com/category/siouan-catawban-language-family,"Siouan-Catawban Language Family Alternate Names: Siouan proper, Western Siouan,Central Siouan, Iowa-Oto-Missouri, Southeastern Siouan, Eastern Siouan, and Catawban. The Siouan languages are a Native American language family of North America, and the second largest indigenous language family in North America, after Algonquian. The Siouan language family is related to the Catawban language ...",Iowa-Oto,Siouan-Catawban
3,Otoe-Missouria Subject Guide,https://mads.si.edu/mads/id/NMNH-NAASubjectGuide_Otoe_Missouria_2024,"Collections included in this guide may be catalogued under any of the following: Oto, Iowa-Oto, Otoe, Otoe-Missouria, Missouri, Missouria, Nyut'chi, and Niutaji. The documentation in the records listed in this guide may be in the Oto language, English, Chiwere, Báxoje-Jíwere-Ñút'achi and/or other languages of the Siouan language family.",Iowa-Oto,Siouan-Catawban
4,"Iowa, Oto, and Omaha phrases extracted from manuscript by William ...",https://www.si.edu/object/archives/components/sova-naa-ms4800-ref104,See more items in MS 4800 James O. Dorsey papers MS 4800 James O. Dorsey papers / Series 1: Siouan-Catawban / 1.1: General Siouan,Iowa-Oto,Siouan-Catawban
5,Siouan Language Group of Native Americans - Legends of America,https://www.legendsofamerica.com/siouan-language/,"The Siouan language once consisted of 17 dialects, many of which are either extinct or severely endangered today. The Catawba, Mandan, Iowa-Oto, Kansa, Osage, Biloxi, Ofo, and Tutelo languages are already extinct.",Iowa-Oto,Siouan-Catawban
6,Siouan Language Family - YouTube,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mX546JJukRI,The Siouan-Catawban Language Family of North America. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siouan_languages http://www.native-languages.org/famsio.htm Crow: https:/...,Iowa-Oto,Siouan-Catawban
7,Delores Harragarra Collection - Sam Noble Museum,https://samnoblemuseum.ou.edu/collections-and-research/native-american-languages/native-american-languages-collections/delores-harragarra-collection/,"The collection features over 200 hours of audio recordings documenting nine languages within the Algonquian, Athabaskan, Kiowa-Tanoan and Siouan-Catawban language families. The materials include songs and dances in Cheyenne, Chippewa, Crow, Dakota, Iowa-Oto, Kiowa-Apache, Northern Tiwa, Ponca and Tewa, with some English.",Iowa-Oto,Siouan-Catawban
8,Jiwere Language - Sam Noble Museum - University of Oklahoma,https://samnoblemuseum.ou.edu/collections-and-research/native-american-languages/map-of-oklahoma-languages/jiwere-language/,"Jiwere is also known as Chiwere, Iowa-Otoe, Otoe-Missouria, Jiwere-Nut'achi, or Baxoje. Iowa, Otoe and Missouria are generally considered dialects of the Jiwere language, which is a member of the Siouan-Catawban language family. However, the Iowa and Otoe-Missouria Tribes are distinct political entities. Jiwere's closest linguistic relative is Ho-Chunk. More distantly related languages ...",Iowa-Oto,Siouan-Catawban
9,Title Of Document - JSTOR,https://www.jstor.org/stable/community.32212466,"SOME PHONOLOGICAL RULES OF IOWA-OTO (SIOUAN) Lila Robinson In 1971-2 on a small faculty research grant at Kansas State University linguistic data was collected for the unpublished ""Iowa/ Dictionary"". The chief informant speaks Southern Iowa and Oto of Only a few speakers of Northern Iowa remain near the Kansas-Nebraska border. Iowa (Ioway/Baxoje) and Oto (Otoe/Chiwere) with the extinct ...",Iowa-Oto,Siouan-Catawban
